<section xml:id="section-subgroups">
  <title>Subgroups</title>
  <p>
    I've already introduced two ways of capturing the structure of a
    group: Caley tables and conjugacy classes. Another way is to
    analyze its subgroups. First, the definition.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>G</m> be a group. A subset <m>H \subset G</m> is a
        <term>subgroup</term> of <m>G</m> is <m>H</m> is itself a
        group under the same binary operation as <m>G</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Equivalently, <m>H</m> is a subgroup if these three things are
        true.
        <ul>
          <li>
            <m>H</m> contains the group identity.
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>H</m> is closed under the binary operation. 
          </li>
          <li>
            For any <m>h \in H</m>, the inverse <m>h^{-1}</m> is also
            in <m>H</m>.
          </li>
        </ul>
        Additionally, if <m>H</m> is a proper subset of <m>G</m> (that
        is, <m>H \neq G</m>), then it is called a <term>proper
        subgroup</term>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    In abstract algebra, the definition of a <sq>sub-object</sq> is
    frequently one of the first definitions. It's pretty natural to see
    what subsets of an object still fulfill the rules of that object.
    Note that the subgroup definition doesn't need to check
    associativity: the associativity of the operation is already known
    from the larger group <m>G</m>. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Let's get to some examples, using the various groups defined so
    far in the course. 
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Let <m>e \in G</m> be the identity element of a group. Then
      <m>\{e\}</m> is always a subgroup. As usual, mathematicians
      love our trivial cases: feel free to check that all the
      requirements of the definition are certainly met by the set
      that only contains the identity. This subgroup will be called
      the <term>trivial subgroup</term>. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Many of the number sets were groups under addition. Since
      smaller numbers sets subsets of the larger number sets, I can
      identify subgroups. <m>\ZZ</m> is a subgroup of <m>\QQ</m>,
      which is a subgroup of <m>\RR</m>, which is a subroup of
      <m>\CC</m>. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Something similar is true under multiplication. <m>\QQ^*</m> is
      a subgroup of <m>\RR^*</m>, which is a subgroup of <m>\CC^*</m>.
      The non-zero integers don't show up here, since they are not a
      group under multiplication. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      One might be tempted to say something like <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m> is a
      subgroup of <m>\ZZ/m\ZZ</m> whenever <m>n \leq m</m>, or at least
      when <m>n</m> is a factor of <m>m</m>. Niether are true. The
      classes in <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m> are integers modulo <m>n</m>, while the
      classes in <m>\ZZ/m\ZZ</m> are integers modulo <m>m</m>. If the
      two moduli are different, when these classes can't really be
      compared and are certainly not part of the same set at all. 
    </p>
    <p>
      That said, I can give some specific examples of subgroups of
      <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m>. For all of these, I leave it to you to verify
      that they are closed under addition, include the identity, and
      have all the inverses they need. 
      <ul>
        <li>
          <m>\{0,2,4,6\}</m> is a subgroup of <m>\ZZ/8\ZZ</m>. In
          general, the even classes are always a subgroup of
          <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m> when <m>n</m> is even. 
        </li>
        <li>
          <m>\{0,3,6,9\}</m> is a subgroup of <m>\ZZ/12</m>. 
        </li>
        <li>
          <m>\{0,5,10,15,20\}</m> is a subgroup  of <m>\ZZ/25</m>.
        </li>
        <li>
          You may be already thinking about various propositions about
          subgroups of <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m> based on these examples: there
          certainly are patterns here. Later in this section, 
          I'll state and prove some propositions about
          subgroups of cyclic groups, which will generalize some of
          the patterns you might see here.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      <m>\{1,9\}</m>, <m>\{1,11\}</m>, <m>\{1,19\}</m>, and
      <m>\{1,3,7,9\}</m> are subgroups of <m>\ZZ/20\ZZ^*</m>. You can
      verify the subgroup properties using the Caley table <xref
      ref="example-caley-z20-units"/>. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      The set of real-coefficient polynomials of degree at most 2,
      is a subgroup of <m>\RR[x]</m>. The identity is the zero
      polynomial, which has degree zero, so it is included. 
      I can never get a higher degree polynomial by adding quadratics,
      and the inverse (under addition) of a quadratic is another
      quadratic. Note that <sq>at most</sq> is important here. I can
      add two quadratics and get a linear polynomial, as in this
      example: <m>x^2 + (-x^2 + x) = x</m>, so I need linear and
      constant polynomials to make sure this subset is closed under
      addition.
    </p>
    <p>
      There was nothing special about 2 in the previous paragraph. The
      set of real-coefficient polynomials od degree at most <m>n</m>,
      for any <m>n \in \NN</m>, is a subgroup of <m>\RR[x]</m>. 
    </p>
    <p>
      This examples work with other coefficients as well: in
      <m>\ZZ[x]</m>, in <m>\QQ[x]</m> and so on. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      There are many subgroups in the function groups, both under
      pointwise operations and under composition. In general, if the
      domain and codomains are fixed, then moving to more restrictive
      properties gives subgroups. For example:
      <me>  
        C^{\infty}(\RR) \subset \ldots \subset C^2(\RR) \subset
        C^1(\RR) \subset C(\RR) 
      </me>
      The continuous functions (under pointwise addition) are the most
      general group in this list. Moving to continuously
      differentiable functions is a restriction, so a subset. But the
      inverse of a continuously differentiable function is still
      continuously differentiable, and likewise for sums of such
      functions. So this is a subgroup. Then being twice continuously
      differentiable is a stronger condition yet, then three times,
      four times, and so on to infinitely differentiable functions. At
      each step, the zero function is still present and the inverses
      and sums stay in the same subset, so these are subgroups all the
      way down. 
    </p>
    <p>
      This can be repeated for any other real domain as well, of
      course. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Under composition, something similar happens. 
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{f: \RR \rightarrow \RR | f \text{ is bijective and
          infinitely differentiable} \} 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \vdots
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \subset \amp \{f: \RR \rightarrow \RR | f \text{ is
          bijective and twice continuously differentiable} \} 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \subset \amp \{f: \RR \rightarrow \RR | f \text{ is
          bijective and continuously  differentiable} \} 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \subset \amp \{f: \RR \rightarrow \RR | f \text{ is
          bijective and continuous} \} 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \subset \amp \{f: \RR \rightarrow \RR | f \text{ is
          bijective} \}
        </mrow>
      </md>
      This is again a cascade of subgroups. Since I am working with
      compositions, I need to assume that these are bijective at each
      step: functions under composition are invertible if and only if
      they are bijective. After that assumption, though, I can impose
      stronger and stronger assumptions to make subgroups. At each step,
      the subgroups are closed under composition, include the identity
      function (which is the identity under composition), and have
      inverses which satisfy the same properties. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      These are all the subgroups of <m>D_4</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{\Id\} 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{\Id, F_1\}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{\Id, F_2\} 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{\Id, F_3\} 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{\Id, F_4\} 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{\Id, R_2\} 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{\Id, R_1, R_2, R_3\} 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{\Id, R_2, F_1, F_3\}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{\Id, R_2, F_2, F_4\}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp D_4
        </mrow>
      </md>
      This can be verified, again, from the Caley table <xref
      ref="table-d4-multiplication"/>. Doing so is a fair bit of work
      (to do this the most naive way would be to test all <m>2^8 =
      256</m> possible subsets to see which satisfied the subgroup
      properties), but for finite groups, it is nice to at least have
      the possibility of exhaustively checking. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      These are all the subgroups of <m>D_5</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{\Id\} 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{\Id, F_1\}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{\Id, F_2\} 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{\Id, F_3\}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{\Id, F_4\}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{\Id, F_5\}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \{\Id, R_1, R_2, R_3, R_4\}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp D_5
        </mrow>
      </md>
      Note that even though there are more elements in <m>D_5</m> than
      <m>D_4</m>, there actually fewer subgroups. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      The matrix groups (classical groups) also nicely fit subgroup
      relations. Here are a few of those chains of subgroups. 
      <md>
        <mrow>
          SO(n) \subset SL(n) \subset GL(n)
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          SO(n) \subset O(n) \subset GL(n) 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          GL(\ZZ,n) \subset GL(\QQ,n) \subset GL(\RR,n) \subset
          GL(\CC,n) 
        </mrow>
      </md>
      All of these subsets are subgroups. The identity is matrix is
      contained in each. Each is closed under matrix multiplication,
      and the inverses remain in the subsets for each of these.
    </p>
    <p>
      The different between the first and second chains of subgroups
      is interesting. <m>SO(n)</m> is a subgroup of both <m>SL(n)</m>
      and <m>O(n)</m>. But there is no subgroup relationship between
      <m>SL(n)</m> and <m>O(0n)</m>. Neither is a subset of the
      either, even though they both lie in <m>GL(n)</m>. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      <m>A_n</m> is a subgroup of <m>S_n</m>. The identity is an even
      permutation, the product of two even permutations remains even,
      and the inverse of an even permutation is even: <m>A_n</m>
      satisfies the subgroup rules. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    Having hopefully given a rich basis of examples, let me move on to
    some definitions that help to understand subgroup structures.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>G</m> be a group and let <m>A \subset G</m> be a
        subset. The <term>subgroup generated by <m>A</m></term> is the
        smallest subgroup of <m>G</m> that contains <m>A</m>. The
        notation for this <m>\langle A \rangle</m>. If <m>A</m> is a
        singleton or a small finite set, then the notation is usually
        <m>\langle a \rangle</m> or <m>\langle a,b,c \rangle</m>
        instead of the more technically correct <m>\langle \{a\}
        \rangle</m> or <m>\langle \{a,b,c\} \rangle</m>. A subgroup
        generated by a single element is a <term>cyclic
        subgroup</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    There are some subtleties with this definition, and with the word
    <sq>generated</sq>. I'm going to address these subtleties with a
    proposition, which I won't prove here for sake of time. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>G</m> be a group and let <m>A \subset G</m> be a
        subset. Then consider the following three constructions.
        <ul>
          <li>
            The subgroup generated by <m>A</m> as I just defined it:
            the smallest subgroup that contains the set <m>A</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            The set of all possible finite products (using whatever
            the group binary operation is, of course) of elements of
            <m>A</m> and their inverses. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The intersection of all subgroups of <m>G</m> which
            contain <m>A</m>. 
          </li>
        </ul>
        These three constructions are identical, and the term
        <sq>subgroup generated by <m>A</m></sq> can be understood in
        any of these three ways. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    The equivalence of the first and the second is important here. The
    first is a nice clear definition: the smallest subgroup that
    contains a subset. But it doesn't actually capture the sense of
    <sq>generated</sq> that informed <xref
    ref="definition-generated"/>. In that definition, generated meant
    that you can get everything in the group (or now subgroup) as
    product of the generators and their inverses. The proposition
    tells me that I can still using <sq>generated</sq> in this
    constructive sense, which is very good. 
  </p>
  <p>
    This is also a good example of constructive and non-constructive
    definitions. The definition of a subgroup generated by a subset
    that I gave is nonconstructive. It makes sense that a minimal
    subgroup containing a set exists, but the definition doesn't give
    you any idea of what that subgroup it nor how to calculate it. But
    the alternative definition, where the subgroups is explicitly the
    things you can get as products of elements of the subset and their
    inverses, actually shows you how to build the subgroup. It
    demonstrates a construction.  
  </p>
  <p>
    This definition is very useful: many of the subgroups we talk
    about in this course will be presented as subgroups generated by
    elements. But we must be careful. Let me return to the dihedral
    example to demonstrate. 
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      These are all the subgroups of <m>D_4</m>, but now written with
      generators. 
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id\} \amp = \langle \emptyset \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id, F_1\} \amp = \langle F_1 \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id, F_2\} \amp = \langle F_2 \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id, F_3\} \amp = \langle F_3 \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id, F_4\} \amp = \langle F_4 \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id, R_2\} \amp = \langle R_2 \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id, R_1, R_2, R_3\}
          \amp = \langle R_1 \rangle
          = \langle R_3 \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \{\Id, R_2, F_1, F_3\}, \amp = \langle R_2, F_1 \rangle
          = \langle R_2, F_3 \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
         \{\Id, R_2, F_2, F_4\}, \amp = \langle  R_2, F_2
         \rangle = \langle  R_2, F_4 \rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
         D_4 \amp = 
         \langle  R_1, F_4 \rangle = 
         \langle  R_3, F_2 \rangle = \text{many others}
        </mrow>
      </md>
      There are a number of useful things to point out from this
      example. First, the same group can be described by different
      sets of generators, as the last three examples show. Second, there
      are several cyclic subgroups of various orders, but not all
      subgroups are cyclic: the last two subgroups require two
      generators. Third and most importantly, it can be tricky to see,
      at a glance, what kind of subgroup is generated by certain
      elements. <m>\langle R_1 \rangle</m> is a subgroup of order 4, but
      <m>\langle R_2 \rangle</m> is a subgroup of order 2. <m>\langle
      R_2,F_4 \rangle</m> is a subgroup of order 4, but <m>\langle
      R_1,F_4 \rangle</m> is actually all of <m>D_4</m>. It's good to
      be careful with subgroups described by generations <mdash/> to
      be hesitant to jump to conclusions about the order of subgroups
      generated by elements. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    I want to define a new group to use for examples, in this and in
    future sections of the course.  
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>quaternion group</term> is the set
        <m>Q = \{1,-1,i,-i,j,-j,k,-k\}</m> with the following Caley
        table.  
        <me>
          \begin{array}{c|cccccccc}
            \cdot \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp i \amp -i \amp j \amp -j \amp k
            \amp -k \\
            \hline
            1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp i \amp -i \amp j \amp -j \amp k \amp
            -k \\[0.2em]
            -1 \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp -i \amp i \amp -j \amp j \amp -k \amp 
            k\\[0.2em]
            i \amp i \amp -i \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp k \amp -k \amp -j \amp 
            j\\[0.2em]
            -i \amp -i \amp i \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp -k \amp k \amp j \amp 
            -j \\[0.2em]
            j \amp j \amp -j \amp -k \amp k \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp i \amp 
            -i\\[0.2em]
            -j \amp -j \amp j \amp k \amp -k \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp -i \amp 
            i\\[0.2em]
            k \amp k \amp -k \amp j \amp -j \amp -i \amp i \amp -1 \amp 
            1\\[0.2em]
            -k \amp -k \amp k \amp -j \amp j \amp i \amp -i \amp 1 \amp 
            -1
          \end{array}
        </me>
        This table is a bit strange to parse. It's easy to remember
        that all of <m>i</m>, <m>j</m> and <m>k</m> square to
        <m>-1</m>, and that multiplying them together in order gives
        the next in alphabet order (looping around from <m>k</m> to
        <m>i</m>). 
        <md>
          <mrow>
            i^2 = j^2 = k^2 = -1 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            ij = k \hspace{1cm} jk = i \hspace{1cm} ki = j 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            ji = -k \hspace{1cm} kj = -i \hspace{1cm} ik = -j 
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        These quaterion relations were first invented by Irish
        mathematician William Rowan Hamilton. There is a bridge
        (called the Broom Bridge) in Dublin with a plaque whereon
        these multiplication rules are written, to commemorate
        Hamilton's invention <mdash/> according to popular mathematical
        myth, Hamilton was walking along the canal by this bridge when
        the idea occured to him. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <example>
    <p>
      These are the subgroups of <m>Q</m>.
      <me>
        \{\Id\}, \langle -1 \rangle, \langle i \rangle, \langle j
        \rangle, \langle k \rangle 
      </me>
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    The first proposition I want to prove has to do with the relative
    sizes of subgroups. In order to prove this proposition, I need a 
    definition and another proposition first. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>G</m> be a group, let <m>H \subset G</m> be a
        subgroup, and let <m>g \in G</m>. A <term>left coset</term> of
        <m>H</m> is a subset <m>gH \subset G</m>. This notation means
        <m>\{gh | h \in H\}</m>: it is the set made by taking one
        fixed group element <m>g</m> and multiplying by all of the
        elements of <m>H</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    I have a few notes about cosets. First, note that a coset is a
    subset. Different group elements <m>g</m> may lead to the same
    coset. As an
    example, consider the subgroup <m>H = 
    \{1,3,7,9\} \subset \ZZ/20\ZZ^*</m>. The coset <m>7H</m> is the
    classes of all four of these elements multiplied by 7: <m>7H =
    \{7, 1, 9, 3\}</m>. This is the same as the coset <m>1H = \{1, 3,
    7, 9\}</m>. (Recall that a set is not ordered <mdash/> these are
    the same set!) A coset is a subset, and there can be several ways
    to define a coset using some group element <m>g</m>. 
  </p>
  <p> 
    Also note that the definition, as usual, is written
    multiplicatively. If the group operation is some other binary
    operation, the cosets will be written with that binary operation.
    For example, I can define the multiples of 10, <m>10\ZZ</m>, as a
    subgroup of the integers. A coset of this subgroup looks like
    <m>(6 + 10\ZZ)</m>: it is some group element added to everything
    in the subgroup, since addition is the group operation. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, I have defined <sq>left</sq> cosets: the multiplication
    by the group element <m>g</m> is on the left. Since groups are
    not, in general, commutative, being on the left is important. This
    whole theory of cosets can be repeated with right cosets: cosets
    of the form <m>Hg</m>. I'm not going to do that, and for the rest
    of this course, when I say <sq>coset</sq>, I will always means
    left coset. But it is good to be aware that the other option was
    possible. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Now, to setup the next proposition, recall from <xref
    ref="definition-partition"/> the idea of a partition of a set. So
    far, partitions have come from equivalence relations, but cosets
    are also a path to a partition. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>G</m> be a finite group. Let <m>H \subset G</m> be a
        subgroup. Then the left cosets of <m>H</m> form a partition of
        <m>G</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        I need to prove that the cosets are disjoint and that all
        elements of the group show up in one of the cosets. I'll start
        with the first part. 
      </p>
      <p>
        To prove that the cosets are disjoint, I can argue that if two
        cosets share a single element, then they must be exactly the
        same coset. So let <m>g_1 H</m> and <m>g_2 H</m> be two cosets
        that share an element <m>k</m>. (By definition, this means
        that there exists <m>h_1 \in H</m> and <m>h_2 \in H</m> such
        that <m>k = g_1h_1</m> and <m>k = g_2h_2</m>.) I need to prove
        these two sets are the same. From <xref
        ref="subsection-miscelaneous-proof"/>, the way to prove that
        two sets are the same is to prove that each is a subset of the
        other. 
      </p>
      <p>
        So let <m>l \in g_1H</m>. This means, by definition, that
        there exists an <m>h_3 \in H</m> such that <m>l = g_1h_3</m>.
        Then consider this calculation
        <me>
          l = g_1h_3 = g_1 h_1 h_1^{-1} h_3 = k h_1^{-1} h_3 = g_2 h_2
          h_1^{-1} h_3 
        </me>
        All of these equalities are justified: the first is just
        multiplying by a subgroup element and it inverse; the second
        is using the first expression for <m>k</m> from the previous
        paragraph, and the third is using the second expression for
        <m>k</m> from above. The result is <m>k = g_2 (h_2 h_1^{-1}
        h_3)</m>; the element in brackets here is a product of things
        in the subgroup <m>H</m>, so it is also in the subgroup.
        Therefore, <m>l</m> is in the coset <m>g_2 H</m>. Since
        <m>l</m> was chosen arbitrarily, this proves that <m>g_1H
        \subset g_2H</m>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        The proof of the reverse inclusion is identical with the
        indices adjusted. This proves that if the two cosets share
        even one element, they must be identital. I conclude, as sets,
        the cosets are disjoint: two different cosets cannot share any
        elements. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, to be a partition, the cosets have to cover <m>G</m>.
        So take an arbitrary <m>g \in G</m>. I can write this as <m>g
        = ge</m> (where <m>e</m> is the standard notation for an identity
        element). But <m>H</m> is a subgroup, so <m>e \in H</m>. This
        means that <m>g = ge \in gH</m>, and I have proved that
        <m>g</m> is in a coset. Therefore, the cosets cover the whole
        group, and I conclude they form a partition. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    Now I can get to an important definition for subgroups. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>H \subset G</m> be a subgroup of a group. Then the
        <term>index of <m>H</m> in <m>G</m></term> is the number of
        <m>H</m> cosets. It is written <m>[G:H]</m>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <example>
    <p>
      <m>H = \{0, 5, 10, 15\}</m> is a subgroup of <m>\ZZ/20\ZZ</m>.
      Here are the cosets of this subgroup:
      <md>
        <mrow>
          0 + H = \{0, 5, 10, 15\}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          1 + H = \{1, 6, 11, 16\}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          2 + H = \{2, 7, 12, 17\}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          3 + H = \{3, 8, 13, 18\}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          4 + H = \{4, 9, 14, 19\}
        </mrow>
      </md>
      There are five cosets, so the index is <m>[\ZZ/20\ZZ: H] =
      5</m>. Note that the elements used to define the coset could
      have be chosen differently: <m>1 + H</m> is the same coset at
      <m>16 + H</m>.
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      The multiples of <m>7</m>, written <m>7\ZZ</m>  are a subgroup
      of the integers. Here are the cosets. 
      <md>
        <mrow>
          0 + 7\ZZ = \{ \ldots, -14, -7, 0, 7, 14, \ldots \}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          1 + 7\ZZ = \{ \ldots, -13, -6, 1, 8, 15, \ldots \}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          2 + 7\ZZ = \{ \ldots, -12, -5, 2, 9, 16, \ldots \}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          3 + 7\ZZ = \{ \ldots, -11, -4, 3, 10, 17, \ldots \}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          4 + 7\ZZ = \{ \ldots, -10, -3, 4, 11, 18, \ldots \}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          5 + 7\ZZ = \{ \ldots, -9, -2, 5, 12, 19, \ldots \}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          6 + 7\ZZ = \{ \ldots, -8, -1, 6, 13, 20, \ldots \}
        </mrow>
      </md>
      There are seven cosets (each of them an infinite set).
      Therefore, the index is <m>[\ZZ: 7\ZZ] = 7</m>. Again,
      different group elements should have been chosen: <m>4 + 7\ZZ =
      74 + \ZZ</m>. Since these are infinite sets, there are in fact
      infinitely many choices! This example also points out that
      infinite groups can have subgroups of finite index, which is
      nice to notice. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      <m>H = \langle R_2 \rangle = \{\Id, R_2\}</m> is a subgroup of
      <m>D_4</m>. Here are its cosets.
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \Id H = \{\Id, R_2\}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          R_1 H = \{R_1, R_3\}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          F_1H = \{F_1, F_3\}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          F_2H = \{F_2, F_4\}
        </mrow>
      </md>
      There are four cosets, so the index is <m>[D_4: \langle R_2
      \rangle] = 4</m>. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    Now I can get to a very valuable result for understanding finite
    groups. This is important enough to be a named theorem.
  </p>
  <theorem>
    <title>Lagrange's Theorem</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>H \subset G</m> be the subgroup of a finite group. The
        the order of <m>H</m> divides the order of <m>G</m> and 
        <me>
          [H:G] = \frac{|G|}{|H|}
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        I use cosets to prove this theorem. Since this is a finite
        group, all the cosets are finite sets. I am first going to
        argue that the cosets all have the same cardinality. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>gH</m> be a coset. Then I claim that the set function
        <m>f: H \rightarrow gH</m> defined by <m>f(h) = gh</m> is a
        bijection. I need to prove surjective and injective. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Surjective is basically by definition. Elements of the coset
        are defined to be <m>gh</m> for <m>h \in H</m>, which is
        exactly the outputs of this function. 
      </p>
      <p>
        For injectivity, assume that <m>f(h_1) = f(h_2)</m>. That means
        that <m>gh_1 = gh_2</m>. But we have cancellation in groups,
        so this mean <m>h_1 = h_2</m>. This is the standard argument
        structure for injectivity, so the function is injective.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since the function is bijective, I conclude that all cosets
        have the same cardinality as <m>H</m> istelf, which also means
        that all cosets have the same cardinality as each other. 
      </p>
      <p>
        So now I have a partition of <m>G</m> into pieces (the cosets)
        and the pieces all have the same cardinality, which is
        <m>|H|</m>. The number of cosets is, by definition,
        <m>[G:H]</m>. I put this all together for this formula: the
        cardinality of <m>G</m> must be the number of cosets
        multiplied by the cardinality of each coset. 
        <me>
          |G| = [G:H]|H|
        </me>
        This formula leads to both conclusions. The left is the order
        of <m>G</m>, and the right shows that <m>|H|</m> is a factor.
        Then dividing by <m>|H|</m> gives the formula for the index. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
  <p>
    This theorem tells us a lot about the subgroups of a finite group.
    For example, if I have a finite group of order 195, then I
    consider the factors of 195: <m>\{1, 3, 5, 13, 15, 39, 65\}</m>.
    The only subsets that can possibly be subgroups are subsets of
    this size. If I am looking for subgroups, I now have a much, much
    narrower search. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Lagrange's theorem also come nice subsequence result. Such results
    are usually called <term>corollaries</term> of a theorem:
    something that is only a small step after a major result and is
    considered, in some sense, a special case of the major result. 
  </p>
  <corollary>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>g \in G</m> be a member of a finite group. Then the
        order of <m>g</m> divides the order of <m>G</m>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        The order of <m>g</m> is the smallest <m>d</m> such that
        <m>g^d = e</m>. Then consider the cyclic subgroup generated by
        <m>g</m>. 
        <me>
          \langle g \rangle = \{e, g, g^2, g^3, g^4 \ldots g^{d-1} \}
        </me>
        This is precisely a subgroup of order <m>d</m>. Lagrange's
        theorem says that the order of a subgroup divides the order of
        the (finite) group, so <m>d</m> divides <m>|G|</m> and the
        proof is completed. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </corollary>
  <corollary>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Any group with prime order <m>p</m> must be cyclic and has no
        subgroups other than itself and the trivial subgroup. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Any element that is not the identity has to have order greater
        than 1 (otherwise <m>g^1 = g = \Id</m>). But the order of an
        element must divide the order of the group, and a prime has no
        other divisors, so the order of <m>g</m> must be <m>p</m>.
        Then again consider the subgroup generated by <m>g</m>.
        <me>
          \langle g \rangle = \{e, g, g^2, g^3, g^4 \ldots g^{p-1} \}
        </me>
        This has <m>p</m> elements, so this must be the whole group. I
        conclude the group is cyclic. Similarly, since the order of
        a subgroup must divide the order of the group and only 1 or
        <m>p</m> divide <m>p</m>, the only subgroups are the entire
        group, order <m>p</m>, or the trivial subgroup, order 1. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </corollary>
</section>
